    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a> created the hereditary Austrian imperial title in 1804, two years before he dissolved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</li><li>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klemens-von-metternich">Metternich</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> led the conservative European order after 1815 and dominated both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</li><li>Defeats in 1859 and 1866 ended Austrian power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and led to the Compromise with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> in 1867.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregor-mendel">Gregor Mendel</a>, an Augustinian friar, presented his pea-plant experiments on heredity in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brünn</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, in 1865; they were largely ignored until they were rediscovered in 1900.</p></aside>
    </section>

