    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon.jpg" alt="Napoleon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacques-Louis_David_-_The_Emperor_Napoleon_in_His_Study_at_the_Tuileries_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jacques-Louis David, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Austrian Empire was proclaimed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a>, the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>, on 11 August 1804. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> had made himself Emperor of the French in May, and the elective Holy Roman crown was visibly failing after the territorial upheavals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichsdeputationshauptschluss">Imperial Recess of 1803</a>, so Francis took a new hereditary title, Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i">Francis I</a>. The title gave a common name to the composite monarchy the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> had built since the sixteenth century: the Austrian hereditary lands, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lands-of-the-bohemian-crown">Lands of the Bohemian Crown</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, the Grand Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, taken in the partitions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Each land kept its own laws, estates and traditions, and the empire was a dynastic union rather than a single state. Defeat by Napoleon at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-austerlitz">Battle of Austerlitz</a> in December 1805 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-pressburg-1805">Peace of Pressburg</a> cost Austria <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vorarlberg">Vorarlberg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetia">Venetia</a>, and the formation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a> in July 1806 made the old empire meaningless. On 6 August 1806 Francis laid down the Holy Roman crown and dissolved the empire, after which he ruled as Emperor of Austria alone.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-ferdinand-northern-railway"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-ferdinand-northern-railway.jpg" alt="Emperor Ferdinand Northern Railway" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-ferdinand-northern-railway">Emperor Ferdinand Northern Railway</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dworzec_G%C5%82%C3%B3wny_-_detal_fasady.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gaj777, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The empire was a mosaic of peoples: Germans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Hungarians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechs">Czechs</a>, Slovaks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruthenians">Ruthenians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a> and Italians, with large Jewish communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. Its population exceeded thirty million by the middle of the century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Roman Catholicism</a> was the dynasty's faith and the established church, although Protestants, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Christians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were tolerated under the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Joseph II</a>. Most subjects were peasants, and in many provinces they owed labour services to noble landlords until the revolution of 1848, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hans-kudlich">Hans Kudlich</a>'s motion to abolish servitude. Industry grew mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a> and Bohemia, with textiles, glass, iron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, while Hungary remained overwhelmingly agrarian and was separated from the western lands by an internal customs line until 1851. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-ferdinand-northern-railway">Emperor Ferdinand Northern Railway</a> began running in 1837, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semmering-railway">Semmering Railway</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> was completed in 1854, and the Southern Railway linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a>, the empire's main port and home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-lloyd">Austrian Lloyd</a> shipping company. Vienna, with several hundred thousand inhabitants, was the political and cultural centre, and in 1857 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph</a> ordered its old fortifications demolished to make way for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ringstrasse">Ringstrasse</a>. Public life under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klemens-von-metternich">Metternich</a> was constrained by censorship, so the comfortable private culture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biedermeier">Biedermeier</a> middle classes flourished instead.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-austerlitz"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-austerlitz.jpg" alt="Battle of Austerlitz" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-austerlitz">Battle of Austerlitz</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_bataille_d%27Austerlitz._2_decembre_1805_(Fran%C3%A7ois_G%C3%A9rard).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">François Gérard, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Austrian army was the dynasty's main instrument of unity, recruited from all its lands and officered largely by a supranational nobility. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> it suffered repeated defeats, at Ulm and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-austerlitz">Austerlitz</a> in 1805 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-wagram">Wagram</a> in 1809, but under Archduke Charles it inflicted on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> his first major defeat in battle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-aspern-essling">Aspern-Essling</a> in May 1809. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> joined the Sixth Coalition in August 1813, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-philipp-prince-of-schwarzenberg">Field Marshal Karl Philipp</a> zu Schwarzenberg commanded the Allied armies that won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leipzig">Battle of Leipzig</a>. After 1815 Austrian troops suppressed revolutions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> in 1821 and intervened repeatedly in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. In 1848–1849 the army saved the monarchy: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-radetzky-von-radetz">Field Marshal Joseph Radetzky</a> defeated Piedmont-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> at Custoza in 1848 and Novara in 1849, and Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-i-prince-of-windisch-gratz">Alfred Windischgrätz</a> retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. Against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, however, Austria needed the help of a Russian army, and the Hungarian forces surrendered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surrender-at-vilagos">Világos</a> in August 1849. Conservative leadership and financial weakness left the army ill-prepared for modern war. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Piedmont defeated it at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-magenta">Magenta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-solferino">Solferino</a> in 1859, and in 1866 the Prussian army, using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breech-loading-rifle">breech-loading rifles</a> and railways, crushed it at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-koniggratz">Battle of Königgrätz</a> on 3 July. In the same war Austria defeated Italy on land at Custoza and at sea at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lissa-1866">Battle of Lissa</a> under Wilhelm von Tegetthoff, but those victories could not offset the decision in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/carlsbad-decrees"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carlsbad-decrees.jpg" alt="Carlsbad Decrees" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/carlsbad-decrees">Carlsbad Decrees</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:German_Conservatism_Flags_Symbol.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hstoops, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The empire was governed as an absolute monarchy from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, but its lands kept separate institutions, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> with its own diet and county administration. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i">Francis I</a> distrusted change and relied on a large bureaucracy, police surveillance and censorship. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klemens-von-metternich">Klemens von Metternich</a>, foreign minister from 1809 and state chancellor from 1821, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> the guardian of the settlement of 1815: he hosted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a>, presided over the German Confederation, in which Austria held the permanent chair of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-convention-german-confederation">Federal Diet</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, and pushed through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlsbad-decrees">Carlsbad Decrees</a> of 1819 against liberal and national agitation. After Francis died in 1835 his son Ferdinand I, who was incapable of ruling, reigned while a State Conference governed. The revolutions of March 1848 drove Metternich into exile and forced the promise of constitutions, and in December 1848 Ferdinand abdicated in favour of his eighteen-year-old nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph I</a>. Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-zu-schwarzenberg">Felix zu Schwarzenberg</a> dissolved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kromeriz">Kremsier</a> parliament in 1849, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sylvester-patent">Sylvester Patent</a> of 1851 restored absolutism. In the following decade of neo-absolutism, associated with the interior minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-von-bach">Alexander von Bach</a>, the government centralised administration, imposed German as the language of officialdom and concluded a concordat with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> in 1855. Defeat in 1859 forced experiments with representation, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-diploma">October Diploma</a> of 1860 and the February Patent of 1861, which created an imperial parliament that Hungarian leaders boycotted.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vienna"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vienna.jpg" alt="Vienna" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schoenbrunn_philharmoniker_2012.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Superbass, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For half a century the Austrian Empire was a central pillar of the European order. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a>, held in its capital in 1814–1815, redrew the map of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and established the system of great-power diplomacy known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concert-of-europe">Concert of Europe</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klemens-von-metternich">Metternich</a>'s name became shorthand for conservative restoration. Austrian predominance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> delayed both national unifications, which when they came were achieved against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. The empire's experience also shaped modern debates about nationality. The Czech historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frantisek-palacky">František Palacký</a> argued in 1848 that if Austria did not exist it would have to be invented, as a protection for the small nations of central Europe, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-slavism">Austro-Slavism</a>, Hungarian liberalism and Italian nationalism all took form in conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. Culturally the period was one of exceptional achievement. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-van-beethoven">Ludwig van Beethoven</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-schubert">Franz Schubert</a> worked in Vienna, the Strauss family made the waltz popular across Europe, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-grillparzer">Franz Grillparzer</a> wrote for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgtheater">Burgtheater</a>. In science, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ignaz-semmelweis">Ignaz Semmelweis</a> showed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna-general-hospital">Vienna General Hospital</a> that handwashing prevented childbed fever, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregor-mendel">Gregor Mendel</a> presented his experiments on plant hybridisation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brünn</a> in 1865, laying the foundations of genetics. The administrative, legal and military institutions of this period, including the General Civil Code of 1811, passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> and, in part, to the successor states of central Europe.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-solferino"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-solferino.jpg" alt="Battle of Solferino" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-solferino">Battle of Solferino</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yvon_Bataille_de_Solferino_Compiegne.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Adolphe Yvon, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The empire's position eroded through the 1850s. By staying neutral but hostile to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> lost the friendship of the power that had rescued it in 1849, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Piedmont-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> combined against it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The war of 1859 ended with defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-solferino">Solferino</a> and the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>, and the expensive army and an unstable currency left the state close to bankruptcy. Rivalry with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> over leadership in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> came to a head after the joint war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in 1864. In 1866 Bismarck's Prussia, allied with Italy, defeated Austria in seven weeks. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-prague-1866">Peace of Prague</a> in August 1866 dissolved the German Confederation and excluded Austria from German affairs, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetia">Venetia</a> passed to Italy through France. Weakened abroad, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph</a> needed a settlement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, whose leaders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferenc-deak">Ferenc Deák</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyula-andrassy">Gyula Andrássy</a> had refused the centralised constitutions of 1860–1861. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-compromise-of-1867">Compromise of 1867</a> recognised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> as an equal partner with its own government and parliament, joined to the remaining lands by the person of the monarch and by common ministries for foreign affairs, war and joint finance. Franz Joseph was crowned King of Hungary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> on 8 June 1867, and the December Constitution of the same year reorganised the western lands. The unitary Austrian Empire thereby became the dual monarchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Austrian Empire gave a single imperial title to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>' many lands from 1804, led the conservative European order after the Congress of Vienna, survived the revolutions of 1848, and was transformed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> in 1867 after defeats by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>.</em></p>

